Every ENet socket in the engine binds to ENET_HOST_ANY (all interfaces), so each Windows test run of kke_tests asks the firewall to let it in. We add a process-wide bind address; the tests set it to 127.0.0.1. Use the bind address. Four edits, nothing else changes:
1. In EnetTransport::host, the line `    address.host = ENET_HOST_ANY;` becomes `    address.host = bindHost();`.
2. In EnetTransport::ensureClientHost, inside the for loop, the line `        address.host = ENET_HOST_ANY;` becomes `        address.host = bindHost();`.
3. In ensureClientHost, the line `    if (!m_host) m_host = enet_host_create(nullptr, 1, 2, 0, 0);` becomes this block (a null address would leave the socket on all interfaces):
```
    if (!m_host) {
        ENetAddress address{};
        address.host = bindHost();
        address.port = 0; // any free port
        m_host = enet_host_create(&address, 1, 2, 0, 0);
    }
```
4. Directly before the line `bool EnetTransport::host(uint16_t port, size_t maxPeers, std::string* error) {` add this function and a blank line after it:
```
std::string EnetTransport::boundAddress() const {
    ENetAddress a{};
    char ip[64] = {};
    if (!m_host || enet_socket_get_address(m_host->socket, &a) != 0 || enet_address_get_host_ip(&a, ip, sizeof ip) != 0) return {};
    return ip;
}
```
